# Platform Functional Testing

- Follow `docs/platform-testing/README.md` for feature fixes, app synchronization and releases.
- Identify the intended web commit, built native bundle, runtime/browser version, account mode and backend/schema version before testing.
- Reproduce the reported action in its affected runtime. A build, screenshot, responsive viewport or mocked bridge test alone does not prove that feature works in the iPhone app.
- Test affected create/edit/delete, Schedule/drag, reload/relaunch persistence and failure feedback. Keep unrelated data unchanged.
- Cover local mode and signed-in mode when supported; verify the real target API contract and required migrations, not only a synthetic transport response.
- For Templates, exercise desktop drag and touch/keyboard Schedule, saved settings, independent task copies, source-template retention and reload.
- App updates require actual WKWebView functional checks in an isolated iPhone/iPad simulator or device, native transport tests, preserved signing/native customizations and built-web-file parity.
- Treat web, installed PWA and bundled native app as separate runtimes. Browser viewport emulation is layout/input evidence, not native or physical Safari verification.
- Check phone/iPad layouts, touch targets, keyboard/focus, reduced motion and supported offline behavior where affected.
- Check account/owner isolation and pending/error states for persistence or authentication changes.
- Use isolated fixtures and dedicated authorized test accounts. Never use real production records as disposable test data or expose credentials in evidence.
- Run proportionate affected-feature checks for small changes; use the full feature/platform baseline for releases, web-bundle syncs and shared storage/API/native-bridge changes.
- Record each required case as PASS, FAIL, BLOCKED or NOT RUN, with runtime/mode/version and evidence. An unavailable runtime or untested physical-device capability is not a pass.
- Distinguish intentionally unsupported capabilities from broken supported features; keep their UI honest. Native alarms and browser notifications have different acceptance checks.
- Resolve failures or state exact remaining limits before claiming completion. Never infer “all platforms work” from one browser or a successful compile.
- Preserve the existing local-first release rule: verification and source preparation do not authorize public deployment or remote migrations.
